Background. An (n+1)-colored graph is a finite multigraph G=(V,E) equipped with an edge-coloring c:E→{0,1,…,n} such that at every vertex exactly one edge of each color is incident (so G is (n+1)-regular and properly edge-colored). To each such graph one associates an n-dimensional polyhedron K(G) by the standard construction: take an n-simplex σ_v for each vertex v∈V whose vertices are labeled by the colors {0,1,…,n}, and for every edge of color i joining v and w, glue σ_v to σ_w by the unique affine identification of their (n−1)-faces opposite the vertex labeled i that preserves the remaining color labels. The graph G is said to represent the topological space |K(G)|; when |K(G)| is a closed PL n-manifold, G is called a gem (graph-encoded manifold), and a contracted gem is a crystallization.

Problem. Provide purely combinatorial / algebraic graph-theoretic characterizations of those (n+1)-colored regular graphs G that represent, via the above construction, manifolds belonging to each of the following fibered families of PL manifolds:
(a) good torus fibrations (PL manifolds admitting a fibration by tori with controlled singular fibers);
(b) surface bundles over a base (with fiber a closed surface);
(c) Seifert fibered 3-manifolds;
(d) PL manifolds equipped with a (locally smooth) torus action;
(e) circle bundles over a closed manifold.

For each class, the characterization should be intrinsic to the colored graph (in terms of its combinatorial structure, automorphisms, partial subgraphs obtained by deleting edges of given color sets, or other graph-theoretic invariants), and it should be both necessary and sufficient for |K(G)| to belong to the prescribed fibered family.

